      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The Birmingham and Southern Railroad arrived in 1913, sparking a brief boom as land sales surged across the town.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://encyclopediaofalabama.org/article/eclectic/",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Eclectic’s first post office opened in 1879, marking the town’s formal entry onto the map of Alabama.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        13
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eclectic%2C_Alabama",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Long before the town existed, the land was owned by Creek tribal member Ho‑Pic‑You‑Che, who received an allotment under the 1832 Treaty of Cusseta.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://encyclopediaofalabama.org/article/eclectic/",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Local lore suggests that the Hernando de Soto expedition may have passed through the area that is now Eclectic in the 1500s.",
